-- title:
--   Lemma 6.9 — double-exponential separation of distinct MDP values
-- statement:
--   For $p\in\{1,2\}$, let $P$ be a player-$p$ MDP with reward function $r$ (values in $[0,1]$) and a succinct discount factor $\lambda=1-2^{-2^{B_\lambda}}$. Let $B_P\in\mathbb N_0$ be such that all rewards and transition probabilities of $P$ have a common denominator at most $2^{2^{B_P}}$, and let
--   $$B_{\mathrm{sep}}=B_\lambda+B_P+2\lceil\log n\rceil+1.$$
--   Then two distinct elements of $\{\mathrm{val}_\lambda(s)\mid s\in S\}\cup\{0,1\}$ differ by at least $2^{-2^{B_{\mathrm{sep}}}}$.
--
--   The separation bounds the exponents of the interval bounds built from the values.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: a finite game G = (S, A, B, δ) with |S| = n and at most m actions per player → states Fin n, actions Fin mA and Fin mB with n, mA, mB ≥ 1, m = max(mA, mB), distributions as nonnegative real vectors summing to one; reason: relabelling of finite sets; nonempty state and action sets are the source game convention
--   - encoding: the probability measure on valid plays Ω_s generated by finite histories, strategies on valid histories → the Ionescu-Tulcea trajectory measure on all sequences in (S × A × B)^ℕ; strategies are defined on all finite stage lists; reason: invalid plays have probability zero and values of strategies on invalid histories never matter, so every expectation and value is unchanged
--   - encoding: a player-p MDP P = (S, A, δ) for p ∈ {1, 2} → a game with the other player's action set Fin 1 (CSG n m 1 for p = 1, CSG n 1 m for p = 2); the theorem is the conjunction of the two cases; reason: the source defines a player-p MDP as the special CSG where the other player has one action
--   - encoding: ⌈log x⌉ (base 2) → Nat.clog 2 x; reason: equal for every positive integer x; all logarithm arguments are positive because the state and action sets are nonempty
--   - encoding: a common denominator at most 2^{2^{B_P}} → for every positive integer Q ≤ 2^{2^{B_P}} such that all probabilities and rewards are integer multiples of 1/Q; reason: the hypothesis "there is such a Q" moved into universally quantified binders; equivalent
-- source:
--   Ali Asadi, Krishnendu Chatterjee, The Computational Complexity of Concurrent Stochastic Games: Exact Value and Approximation, STOC 2027 submission #285 (October 2026), SHA-256 5cff187cf6b230b3bdfe540f2832f824d1ddb6acb43239fbf469d37c43b0d461, https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-1/285.pdf, Lemma 6.9, printed p. 25 (PDF p. 27)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ConcurrentStochasticApprox_Approx_Game
open Finset

namespace ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx

/-- **Lemma 6.9 (Separation of values).** For a player-`p` MDP `P` (`p ∈ {1,2}`) with reward `r`
and succinct discount factor `λ = 1 - 2^{-2^{Bλ}}`, let `B_P ∈ ℕ₀` be such that all rewards and
transition probabilities have a common denominator at most `2^{2^{B_P}}`, and let
`B_sep = Bλ + B_P + 2⌈log n⌉ + 1`. Then two distinct elements of `{val_λ(s) | s ∈ S} ∪ {0, 1}`
differ by at least `2^{-2^{B_sep}}`. -/
theorem lemma_6_9 :
    (∀ {n m : ℕ} (P : CSG n m 1) (r : Reward n m 1), IsReward r → ∀ (Blam BP Q : ℕ), 0 < Q →
      Q ≤ 2 ^ (2 ^ BP) → HasCommonDenom P r Q →
        ∀ x ∈ Set.range (discValue P r (succinct Blam)) ∪ {0, 1},
        ∀ y ∈ Set.range (discValue P r (succinct Blam)) ∪ {0, 1}, x ≠ y →
          (1 / 2 : ℝ) ^ (2 ^ (Blam + BP + 2 * Nat.clog 2 n + 1)) ≤ |x - y|) ∧
    (∀ {n m : ℕ} (P : CSG n 1 m) (r : Reward n 1 m), IsReward r → ∀ (Blam BP Q : ℕ), 0 < Q →
      Q ≤ 2 ^ (2 ^ BP) → HasCommonDenom P r Q →
        ∀ x ∈ Set.range (discValue P r (succinct Blam)) ∪ {0, 1},
        ∀ y ∈ Set.range (discValue P r (succinct Blam)) ∪ {0, 1}, x ≠ y →
          (1 / 2 : ℝ) ^ (2 ^ (Blam + BP + 2 * Nat.clog 2 n + 1)) ≤ |x - y|) := by sorry

end ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx
